<h2>An appeal narrowed to sentence</h2>
<p>The trial court — Additional Sessions Judge, Saket, in a sessions case arising from FIR No. 457/2015, PS Malviya Nagar — convicted the appellant on 19 July 2024 and sentenced him on 20 November 2025 to eight years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment with a Rs. 15,000 fine on each count, concurrent, with set-off. At the hearing on 4 November 2026, counsel gave up the merits challenge, and the appeal proceeded on sentence alone: a first-time offender aged about 32, over six years of actual custody, satisfactory jail conduct across two nominal rolls, nearly five years on bail without misuse, and dependent parents. The complainant opposed any reduction, citing a fractured right temporal bone that required surgery, continuing fear for her safety, and compensation recommended but never received.</p>

<h2>How the Court balanced it</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Mitigation accepted.</strong> Age, clean antecedents, custody already undergone and jail conduct justified reducing each sentence from eight years to seven.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The floor held.</strong> Section 397 IPC prescribes a minimum of seven years where a deadly weapon is used or grievous hurt caused. <em>&ldquo;The Court cannot, in the ordinary exercise of sentencing discretion, award a sentence below that minimum.&rdquo;</em></div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Proportionality cuts both ways.</strong> Soman v. State of Kerala and State of Punjab v. Bawa Singh were applied against undue leniency; the violence here was sustained and inside the victim&rsquo;s home.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The victim&rsquo;s interests addressed.</strong> DLSA (South) directed to decide her compensation claim, preferably within six weeks; liberty to seek police protection recorded.</div>
</div>

<h2>The operative result</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Head</th><th>Trial court</th><th>On appeal</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Conviction — S.307; S.392 r/w S.397 IPC</td><td>Convicted</td><td>Affirmed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sentence on each count</td><td>8 years&rsquo; RI</td><td>7 years&rsquo; RI, concurrent, with set-off</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Fines</td><td>Rs. 15,000 each count; 2 months&rsquo; SI in default</td><td>Unchanged</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Release on time served</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Declined: custody plus remission fell short of the minimum</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Takeaways for sentence appeals</h2>
<p>Mandatory-minimum provisions quietly decide more appeals than headline doctrines do. Where Section 397 IPC — or its BNS successor, Section 311 — is in play, the realistic target of a sentence appeal is the gap between the sentence imposed and the statutory floor, not release on time served. Mitigation of the kind marshalled here is well spent on that gap, on fines and default terms, and on ancillary directions; it cannot pierce the floor itself, and arguments that invite the court to do so tend to weaken the credibility of the rest. Equally notable is the growing practice, visible in this judgment, of addressing victim compensation and safety within the appellate order rather than leaving them to follow-up litigation.</p>
<div class="note">Sentencing outcomes are fact-specific. The reduction here rested on mitigating circumstances personal to the appellant and does not set a tariff. Case details are as recorded in the judgment dated 23 November 2026.</div>
